@@s001 IML Atlas
@@s002 <span>ITU</span><span>autumn 2026</span>
@@s003 <span class="blob">IML</span>
@@s004 atlas of Introduction to Machine Learning
@@s005 We start from <b>vectors</b> and arrive at <b>neural networks</b>, always keeping the same question: which <b>weights</b> make a model consistent with the data? Maths by hand first, NumPy after.
@@s006 The course on one screen
@@s007 Exam
@@s008 <b>Individual oral, up to 30 minutes</b>. You draw one question at random (dice) out of 12 · <a href="esame/formato.html">how it works</a>
@@s009 When
@@s010 official window <b>11–19 January 2027</b>; your slot comes out in the published schedule <b>before Christmas</b>
@@s011 To be admitted
@@s012 2 assignments · 1 presentation · 2 opponent sessions · 1–2 in-class tests
@@s013 In-class test
@@s014 week <b>40</b> (lecture 6), in the first 30–45 min of the lecture. Resit in week <b>41</b>, before lecture 7. Two attempts, then you are out.
@@s015 Assignment
@@s016 group work, handed in on LearnIT<br>1 · Gaze Estimation (from S6)<br>2 · PCA for shape generation (from S11)
@@s017 Workload
@@s018 about <b>10–11.5 hours a week</b>
@@s019 Teacher
@@s020 Dan Witzner Hansen
@@s021 Books
@@s022 <i>A Hands-on Introduction to ML</i> (Shah) = SH<br><i>Introduction to Linear Algebra</i> 6ed (Strang) = ST<br>course notes (Witzner Hansen) = DW
@@s023 Tools
@@s024 Anaconda · Python 3.11 · NumPy · scikit-learn · PyTorch · Grasple
@@s025 Material
@@s026 Git repo <code>IML2026</code> + LearnIT<br>exercises in <code>vscode/</code> or <code>jupyterlab/</code>
@@s027 The week trap
@@s028 The <b>exercises</b> of week N concern the <b>lecture of week N-1</b>. The exam questions say "Exercise Week N" and refer to the <b>folder</b> <code>WN</code>, not to the title of the lecture.
